<DHWEINSTEIN> is here.
<[Dave] DHWEINSTEIN> Answer: Controlled Reference Set
<[Hadil] H.SABBAGH> First, I agree that most people use floating point...
<[Hadil] H.SABBAGH> incorrectly.  I was trained
<[Hadil] H.SABBAGH> in grad school on the use of floating point.
<[Hadil] H.SABBAGH> It is non-trivial...
<[Hadil] H.SABBAGH> However, just because it is in the standard...
<[Hadil] H.SABBAGH> does not require people to use it...
<[Hadil] H.SABBAGH> and people who MUST use it...
<[Dave] DHWEINSTEIN> But if it is in the required standard, then standard 
systems must contain it, which takes space in embedded systems which don't
need it.
<[Hadil] H.SABBAGH> can be assured that it will work in an understandable 
manner...
<[Dave] DHWEINSTEIN> Which is why I say in the controlled reference set. If you 
include it, it should work in XXX manner, but you need not include it.
<[Hadil] H.SABBAGH> That would be the best way.  I doubt that there are many
<[Hadil] H.SABBAGH> new embedded systems w/o some floating point.
<[Dave] DHWEINSTEIN> Oh there are many controller level applications which will 
*not* need floating point.
<[Dave] DHWEINSTEIN> (i.e. device controllers)
<[Gary] GARY-S> You would champion Jack Brown's recommendation that X3/J14 
adopt IEEE fp I assume, Hadil ? Right ?
<[Hadil] H.SABBAGH> I concur.  Controlled reference is best here.  I have
<[Hadil] H.SABBAGH> Re IEEE: I am actually in favor of...
<[Hadil] H.SABBAGH> a floating point standard a la ADA...
<[Dave] DHWEINSTEIN> I would agree. Use IEEE FP. No sense in being nonstandard 
for the sake of being nonstandard. Saves my having to write glue code.
